      "comment_id": "Ugwl2cofVZZNYHwhqah4AaABAg.AZz8HPVhdxcA_02v5qL4-O",
      "text": " @EdenHavaov  That criticism completely misses the point. The experiment was not trying to prove that a rope of that thickness could hold a person. Everyone already knows ordinary rope can do that. The point was to test whether real transgenic spider silk could support a human during an actual swing. Calling for one microscopic strand is not revolutionary. It is reckless and scientifically meaningless. The silk used was already thin enough to cut into the person’s fingers because the load was concentrated over such a small area. Making it even thinner would increase that problem and eliminate any sensible safety margin. Spider silk is impressive because of its strength relative to its weight, its toughness, and the difficulty of producing it at scale. The test demonstrated those properties using a real human load, not a fantasy stunt designed to fail for clicks.",
